WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.400
The "Spider-Verse" is bigger than ever before in&nbsp;
the Oscar-winning movie's first sequel. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.360
thought "Into the Spider-Verse" was dense with&nbsp;
Easter eggs, hold on to your web-shooters – you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.980
ain't seen nothing yet. Warning: spoilers&nbsp;
ahead for Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.400
Though "Into the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
functions as a cohesive narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.420
"Across the Spider-Verse" is the first&nbsp;
part of a completed story, with "Beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.380
the Spider-Verse" set to pick up where it&nbsp;
leaves off. However, "Across the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.760
continues to use a lot of the film language&nbsp;
the original established. One of those motifs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.320
is the use of comic book covers to establish new&nbsp;
characters, as well as catch up with older ones.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.600
"Alright people, let's do this one last time."

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:44.700
The film opens with narration from Gwen Stacy of&nbsp;
Earth-65, examining her backstory as Spider-Woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.500
and teasing her betrayal of Miles later on. Many&nbsp;
of those who watched "Into the Spider-Verse" will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.700
recognize the similarities between this opening&nbsp;
sequence and the introduction of Chris Pine's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.620
Peter Parker in the first film. Some fans may&nbsp;
notice that Stacy's comic book cover drops on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.460
top of the existing covers that were shown&nbsp;
in "Into the Spider-Verse." The trend also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.420
continues for the re-introduction of Miles,&nbsp;
as well as the introduction of new characters.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.380
Furthermore, some of these comic book covers&nbsp;
allude to the characters' narrative history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.080
For Miles' first proper meeting with Miguel&nbsp;
O'Hara, otherwise known as Spider-Man 2099, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.360
comic book cover that flashes on screen directly&nbsp;
references the first issue of "Spider-Man 2099."

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.940
Miles Morales remains the protagonist of "Across&nbsp;
the Spider-Verse," but the extended opening of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.960
the film centers the narrative on Gwen Stacy&nbsp;
following the events of "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.240
When she's not slinging webs and avoiding&nbsp;
captivity, Gwen also shreds on the drum kit, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.740
serves as the opening image of the film. After&nbsp;
she's finally interrupted mid-solo, comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.540
fans will notice her bass drum, which is labeled&nbsp;
"The Mary Janes." Obviously, this fictional band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.280
name is a direct reference to Mary Jane Watson,&nbsp;
who is Peter Parker's other notable love interest.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.600
However, Gwen's involvement in The Mary&nbsp;
Janes dates back to her comic book origins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.340
The band made their debut in Spider-Gwen's&nbsp;
introductory comic, "Edge of Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.800
released in 2014. In that comic, her fellow&nbsp;
band members are established as Betty Brant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.000
a version of Mary Jane Watson who later&nbsp;
becomes Carnage, and Gloria Grant.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.500
Over the years, many actors have taken on&nbsp;
characters in the "Spider-Man" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.140
but arguably only one performer has redefined an&nbsp;
iconic antagonist of Peter Parker's. That actor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.180
none other than J.K. Simmons, who will forever be&nbsp;
remembered as J. Jonah Jameson of The Daily Bugle.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.100
"Crap. Crap. Mega crap. I'll&nbsp;
give you $200 for all of them."

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.060
After appearing in Sam Raimi's original&nbsp;
trilogy, fans of Spider-Man were thrilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.820
to see Simmons reprise his role in&nbsp;
a mid-credits scene for "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.200
Far From Home." Simmons then played a small&nbsp;
role as Peter Parker's loudest critic in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.920
follow-up, "No Way Home," and makes a&nbsp;
comeback via voiceover in "Across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.640
Spider-Verse," protesting the actions of&nbsp;
Spider-People throughout the multiverse.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.580
The character even briefly&nbsp;
appears in the flesh — or rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.760
brick — during one sequence that takes&nbsp;
place in the world of LEGO Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.960
which is the only time in the film Peter&nbsp;
Parker's work at The Daily Bugle is referenced.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.520
Gwen Stacy's backstory is only briefly&nbsp;
alluded to in "Into the Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.880
as her character introduction explains&nbsp;
how her best friend, Peter Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.960
became The Lizard and was subsequently&nbsp;
defeated by Gwen in battle. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.040
the opening of "Across the Spider-Verse" delves&nbsp;
deeper into the characters and what led to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.360
moment, which all originates in Gwen Stacy's&nbsp;
introduction as Spider-Gwen in Marvel Comics.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.920
After getting bitten by a radioactive spider,&nbsp;
Gwen begins living a double life as Spider-Woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.200
using her powers of super-strength to intimidate&nbsp;
Peter Parker's bullies on the side. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.760
fed up with being picked on, Parker downs The&nbsp;
Lizard's serum, turning himself into a monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.560
that attacks their school prom as an act of&nbsp;
revenge against his bullies. Gwen unknowingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.780
kills Peter in the subsequent fight, and runs away&nbsp;
when her father, Captain Stacy, shows up at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.620
scene of the crime to find Spider-Woman&nbsp;
standing over a murdered Peter Parker.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.760
Although this is established in "Into the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" as Gwen's reasoning for remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.020
distant from others, its explanation in "Across&nbsp;
the Spider-Verse" follows the arc from her debut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.940
comic. Some of the sequences in this extended&nbsp;
opening even mirror panels from the comic, such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.340
as one moment when Peter injects himself with Dr.&nbsp;
Connors' serum. Peter's final words to Spider-Gwen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.020
during this scene are also directly taken from&nbsp;
the comic, revealing he knew her secret identity.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.460
The world of the MCU has been focused&nbsp;
on the concept of the multiverse lately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.480
from the bureaucracy policing the flow of&nbsp;
time in "Loki," to the introduction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.920
next Avengers-level threat in the multiversal&nbsp;
conqueror Kang. However, "Into the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.700
was the first Marvel film to really delve&nbsp;
into the comic book origins of the multiverse.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.540
The multiverse also played a major role&nbsp;
in another Spider-Man film, "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.860
No Way Home," in which Tom Holland's Peter&nbsp;
Parker becomes the target of super-villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.820
from the Sonyverse after convincing Doctor&nbsp;
Strange to toy with the memories of everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.960
on Earth. These events seem to be obliquely&nbsp;
referenced in "Across the Spider-Verse" by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.820
Miguel O'Hara during an early scene in the film&nbsp;
as he describes the instability of the multiverse.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.800
"Don't even get me started on Doctor Strange&nbsp;
and the little nerd from Earth-199999."

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.260
Ganke Lee, in Marvel Comics, is Miles Morales'&nbsp;
best friend as well as one of the few people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.040
who knows his secret identity. However,&nbsp;
the character's appearance in "Into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.700
Spider-Verse" was as a minor non-speaking&nbsp;
role, seen most notably when he walks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.480
in on Miles and the other Spider-People&nbsp;
hiding in their shared dorm room. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.960
Ganke makes a slightly more prominent&nbsp;
appearance in "Across the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.340
when Miles stops in his dorm after capturing&nbsp;
The Spot and asks Ganke to call the cops.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:22.800
During the brief moment between Miles and Ganke,&nbsp;
Ganke expresses disinterest in being Miles' "guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.280
in the chair," which results in him not&nbsp;
doing anything when The Spot eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.900
escapes captivity. For fans of the Tom Holland&nbsp;
Spider-Man movies, the phrase "guy in the chair"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.580
will sound a little familiar, as it's said&nbsp;
many times by Peter Parker's friend Ned Leeds.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.340
"Hey, can I be your guy in the chair?"

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:38.940
"What?"

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.740
"You know how there's a guy with a headset&nbsp;
telling the other guy where to go?"

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.520
This Easter egg also references some&nbsp;
connective tissue between Ganke Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.200
and the MCU's Ned Leeds— the latter looks&nbsp;
visually much closer to Ganke Lee in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.080
comics than he does to the Ned Leeds&nbsp;
that eventually becomes the Hobgoblin.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.240
Phil Lord and Christopher Miller have a long&nbsp;
history of producing and creating animated films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.340
including "Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs"&nbsp;
and "The Mitchells vs. the Machines." The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.940
also co-produced "Into the Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;
and returned to help pen the scripts for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.720
both "Across the Spider-Verse" and&nbsp;
2024's "Beyond the Spider-Verse."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.380
Given their greater creative input on the&nbsp;
second and third films of the trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.940
Lord and Miller saw an opportunity to pay tribute&nbsp;
to one of their biggest successes in animation.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.620
That success is "The LEGO Movie," which&nbsp;
the creative duo co-wrote and co-directed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.600
was released in 2014 to glowing reviews&nbsp;
for its stop-motion-style animation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:34.860
storytelling. The two also co-wrote&nbsp;
and produced the film's 2019 sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.960
"The LEGO Movie 2: The Second Part," as well&nbsp;
as producing "The LEGO Batman Movie," "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.920
LEGO Ninjago Movie," and spin-off&nbsp;
Cartoon Network series "Unikitty!".

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.760
In "Across the Spider-Verse," Lord and Miller&nbsp;
go back to their LEGO roots for a moment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.240
the film when The Spot, while jumping&nbsp;
between multiverses, ends up creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.920
havoc in the animated world of LEGO Spider-Man.&nbsp;
Slipping away from his job at The Daily Bugle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.480
LEGO Spider-Man himself contacts Miguel&nbsp;
O'Hara and the Spider-Society to alert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.860
them to the danger of The Spot, setting off&nbsp;
many of the events of the film. Though it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.520
a moment mostly played for laughs, it's a fun&nbsp;
Easter egg for Lord and Miller's past work.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.620
Lord and Miller's work isn't the only thing&nbsp;
referenced during The Spot's multiverse-jumping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.340
in "Across the Spider-Verse." When The Spot&nbsp;
crashes into the LEGO Spider-Man world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:22.080
he also mistakenly ends up in the live-action&nbsp;
Sony-Verse, where he has a brief interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.920
with Mrs. Chen, played by Peggy Lu, the&nbsp;
owner of a convenience store that Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.240
Hardy's Eddie Brock frequents in "Venom" and&nbsp;
its sequel, "Venom: Let There Be Carnage."

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.620
Hilariously, Mrs. Chen is unfazed by the&nbsp;
presence of The Spot, which may have something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.980
to do with the fact that she is briefly a&nbsp;
symbiote host in "Let There Be Carnage."

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.820
While hanging out at night with&nbsp;
his Uncle Aaron, Miles is, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.020
many Spider-People across the multiverse,&nbsp;
bitten by a radioactive spider. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:50.460
what makes Miles an anomaly is that his&nbsp;
spider came to his homeworld of Earth-1610&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.320
from a different universe, Earth-42, with&nbsp;
Miles' acquisition of powers resulting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.280
the death of his universe's Peter Parker&nbsp;
and the instability of the multiverse.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.080
Though this fact plays an important role in why&nbsp;
Miguel O'Hara targets Miles, as well as why Miles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.000
himself ends up on Earth-42 at the end of the&nbsp;
film, the number on that spider is no coincidence.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.140
Miles Morales co-creator Brian Michael&nbsp;
Bendis numbered the radioactive spider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.920
as a nod to the legendary baseball&nbsp;
player Jackie Robinson, who wore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.240
number 42. The number pops up in a number&nbsp;
of places throughout "Into the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:25.560
and Miles can be seen wearing a jersey with&nbsp;
the number 42 in "Across the Spider-Verse."

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:30.240
The number 42 also holds additional meaning&nbsp;
for sci-fi fans. In "The Hitchhiker's Guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.840
to the Galaxy," 42 is established as&nbsp;
the answer to, as the book states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.900
"the ultimate question of life,&nbsp;
the universe, and everything."

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.160
"Yes, yes, I've thought it over&nbsp;
quite thoroughly. It is. It's 42."

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.120
While Miguel thinks that Miles' status as&nbsp;
a universe-ending anomaly is a problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.340
maybe it means that he's the answer instead.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.420
In the history of "Spider-Man" as a&nbsp;
film franchise, there is probably no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:56.460
scene as iconic as the big romantic moment&nbsp;
in Sam Raimi's 2002 film. You know the one:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.000
After rescuing Kirsten Dunst's Mary&nbsp;
Jane Watson from a group of muggers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.140
in the rain, an upside-down Tobey Maguire&nbsp;
passionately kisses his high school crush.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.400
While few romantic pairings have been able to hold&nbsp;
a candle to Maguire and Dunst in "Spider-Man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.300
Miles and Gwen's flirtations in "Across&nbsp;
the Spider-Verse" come pretty close.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.920
Before Gwen disappears into a portal to&nbsp;
chase down The Spot, she turns around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.480
unknowingly face-to-face with an upside-down,&nbsp;
invisible Miles. Though they don't end up locking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.500
lips, it's still a nice homage to one of the&nbsp;
most iconic scenes in "Spider-Man" history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:28.740
and sets up their romantic tension&nbsp;
to continue in the next film.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:33.060
Aunt May isn't as present in "Across&nbsp;
the Spider-Verse" as she was in "Into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.600
the Spider-Verse," with Miles establishing&nbsp;
early in the film that she moved to Florida.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.740
Though this seems like a random fact, it&nbsp;
actually has some basis in Aunt May's comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.700
book origins. The Aunt May that appears in the&nbsp;
Miles Morales comics has family that lives in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.840
Florida, making her move there in "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" potentially a homecoming for her.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.920
Of course, Florida is also a popular&nbsp;
state for elderly citizens to retire in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.180
so maybe we're reading too much into Aunt&nbsp;
May's choice of new destination. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.340
in the tangled web of the "Spider-Verse" films,&nbsp;
no details go unnoticed, so it's possible that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.700
Aunt May has her own adventure ahead of&nbsp;
her when she arrives in the sunshine state.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.320
"Into the Spider-Verse" was heralded as an&nbsp;
outstanding tribute to the history of Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.060
Comics. Not only do the different characters of&nbsp;
the films embody differing comic book aesthetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:19.020
but the film's narrative also cleverly&nbsp;
utilizes comic book tropes to enhance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.200
the storytelling. One example of&nbsp;
this is through its use of comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.320
book flourishes to make the film feel&nbsp;
like a comic book page brought to life.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.020
In "Across the Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;
there are many moments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.340
utilize "Editor's Notes" which briefly&nbsp;
flash on-screen to, as they do in comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:36.240
provide context for the audience on&nbsp;
characters, cultural differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.040
and unexplained lore. However, one Editor's&nbsp;
Note in "Across the Spider-Verse" makes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.460
callback to a beloved character from&nbsp;
"Into the Spider-Verse": Spider-Ham.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:48.900
During the confrontation between the Renaissance&nbsp;
Vulture and Gwen Stacy, Miguel O'Hara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:54.120
and Jessica Drew, O'Hara comments on Vulture's use&nbsp;
of "Hammerspace." According to an Editor's Note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:58.860
"Hammerspace" refers to a cartoon character's&nbsp;
ability to pull objects, such as a large hammer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.580
out of their pockets seemingly from thin&nbsp;
air. Obviously, those who have seen "Into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:07.260
the Spider-Verse" recognize this as an ability&nbsp;
possessed by Spider-Ham, who actually does pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.860
a comically-large hammer out of his pocket&nbsp;
during one of the film's climactic scenes.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.620
In 2018, two major Spider-events happened:&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse" hit theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:22.020
and Insomniac Games released "Spider-Man" for the&nbsp;
PlayStation 4. Since then, Insomniac has developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.820
several expansions and sequels, including&nbsp;
2020's "Spider-Man: Miles Morales" which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:31.260
re-tells the origin story of the "Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
protagonist and his partnership with Peter Parker.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.280
Naturally, the Insomniac Games iterations&nbsp;
of "Spider-Man" make multiple appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.580
in "Across the Spider-Verse." At&nbsp;
first, Miles' roommate Ganke Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:42.660
can be seen playing "Spider-Man" on his TV&nbsp;
instead of writing an essay during several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.740
early scenes in the film. However, the more&nbsp;
prominent cameo comes way later in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.720
when Miles is being taken to meet Spider-Man&nbsp;
2099 in the Spider-Society's headquarters.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.980
At one point, the Insomniac Spider-Man,&nbsp;
as well as Insomniac's Miles Morales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.420
appear among the residents of Nueva York, in&nbsp;
a full CGI render, just like in the games.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:06.600
The climax of "Across the Spider-Verse" comes when&nbsp;
Miles Morales is taken to Nueva York on Earth-928,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:10.440
where Miguel O'Hara has gathered&nbsp;
the Spider-Society. The sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:14.220
where Miles Morales is introduced to this&nbsp;
society is filled with more cameos than one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.480
audience member would be able to spot in one&nbsp;
sitting. Predictably, most of these alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.860
Spider-People originate from comic books or other&nbsp;
official Marvel-produced "Spider-Man" content.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.220
Among some of the highlights are&nbsp;
Otto Octavius's Superior Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:30.480
while Alfred Molina cameos as Doc Ock&nbsp;
through archival use of his voice from "No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.800
Way Home." Other Spider-People that appear&nbsp;
include Spider-Doppelganger, Iron Spider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:41.940
Julia Carpenter's Spider-Woman, Peter Parkedcar,&nbsp;
Ultimate Tarantula, and Spider-Man 2211.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.620
"Do I have web on my face? What's the deal?"

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.680
One of the most surprising cameos&nbsp;
in "Across the Spider-Verse" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.220
been a long time in the making. At one&nbsp;
point during Miles' exploration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:57.240
Spider-Society's headquarters, he meets an&nbsp;
alternate version of his uncle Aaron Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:02.280
aka The Prowler. But this version of Aaron&nbsp;
Davis is played by a live-action Donald Glover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:07.440
who originally debuted the character in the MCU's&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Homecoming" as a low-level criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.880
whom Tom Holland's Peter Parker interrogates for&nbsp;
information about the Vulture. What might make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:16.020
fans extra excited is that, this time,&nbsp;
Glover's actually wearing the costume.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:20.400
Although a deleted post-credits scene in&nbsp;
"Homecoming" teased Glover's Aaron Davis' close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:24.720
relationship with Miles Morales, the character&nbsp;
has yet to re-appear in the film franchise.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.980
"Yeah, sorry, Miles, I'm not&nbsp;
gonna make it. I'm just stuck."

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.160
While the appearance of Glover&nbsp;
is enough to satisfy many fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.000
it also serves as an Easter egg to the&nbsp;
origin of Miles Morales as a character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.280
According to comic book creator Brian Michael&nbsp;
Bendis, Miles' design was inspired by Glover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.320
who was the subject of an online fan campaign&nbsp;
to get him cast as Peter Parker in "The Amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.040
Spider-Man." It won't be the last time you'll&nbsp;
see Glover in a Spider-Man related film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:55.080
either. The "Atlanta" creator is also set to&nbsp;
star in a future Sonyverse film as the minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.680
comic book character Hypno Hustler,&nbsp;
a frequent antagonist of Spider-Man.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:04.500
The multiverse has played a major role in the&nbsp;
on-screen Marvel universe lately, largely thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:08.760
to "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse." In the&nbsp;
years following the end of the Infinity Saga in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:13.140
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Marvel characters&nbsp;
have begun exploring the multiverse, beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:18.720
with the 2021 Disney+ series "Loki," which centers&nbsp;
on the god of mischief being recruited by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.640
mysterious Time Variance Authority, whose goal is&nbsp;
to maintain the sanctity of the Sacred Timeline.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:28.860
In the first season finale of "Loki," Loki and his&nbsp;
female variant Sylvie bear witness to the Sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:34.020
Timeline beginning to split as a result of their&nbsp;
confrontation with He Who Remains. That conception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:37.980
of the multiverse features a blue tree-like&nbsp;
timestream branching off into different paths.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.640
When Miguel O'Hara explains to Miles Morales&nbsp;
how the multiverse works in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.660
Across the Spider-Verse," this familiar&nbsp;
imagery makes a return via a hologram.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.520
At this point, Sony has gotten a little obsessed&nbsp;
with making reference to one popular "Spider-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:55.320
meme in their films. In the post-credits scene&nbsp;
of "Into the Spider-Verse," Miguel O'Hara is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.320 --> 00:15:01.680
introduced as Spider-Man 2099 as he travels to the&nbsp;
universe of the 1967 "Spider-Man" animated series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:05.940
recreating the meme image of two Spider-Men&nbsp;
pointing at each other in an alley.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:09.180
The meme was again referenced on the&nbsp;
set of "No Way Home" when the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:13.140
actors also recreated the image during a&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes costume fitting. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.480
with the release of "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse," the meme makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:20.160
its on-screen appearance yet another time,&nbsp;
albeit under slightly different circumstances.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:24.780
After learning of the Spider-Society's intention&nbsp;
to let Miles' father be killed by The Spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:29.280
Miles makes an escape from their facility as&nbsp;
O'Hara orders everyone to "stop Spider-Man."

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:30.930
"You?"

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:31.800
"Me?"

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:32.395
"You?"

00:15:32.395 --> 00:15:32.820
"Me?"

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:36.900
Even the casting choices in "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" contain Easter eggs. Among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.280
the varied voice cast, there are also two members&nbsp;
of The Lonely Island, a sketch troupe that gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:45.720
notoriety on "Saturday Night Live" for creating&nbsp;
viral Digital Shorts like "Lazy Sunday" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:50.040
"The Shooting." Jorma Taccone, who previously&nbsp;
voiced several minor characters in "Into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.840
Spider-Verse," appears early on as a variant&nbsp;
of the Vulture inspired by Leonardo da Vinci.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:59.280
Alongside Taccone, Andy Samberg also makes&nbsp;
an appearance in the film as Ben Reilly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:04.560
a.k.a. Scarlet-Spider, a moody variant who&nbsp;
serves as a high-up member of Miguel O'Hara's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:10.380
Spider-Society. Reilly, a clone of Peter&nbsp;
Parker, made his comic book debut in 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:14.760
before he later adopted the Scarlet-Spider&nbsp;
persona in the '90s. During his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:19.200
appearance in the film, Samberg's Reilly is&nbsp;
seen in Nueva York contemplating his tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:23.760
backstory before later joining O'Hara&nbsp;
in hunting down Morales on Earth-1610.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:27.480
Samberg's appearance in particular may&nbsp;
be an Easter egg to his work on "Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:31.080
Night Live," as the comedian appeared on&nbsp;
several occasions dressed as Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:35.460
hanging upside down. One of those times was&nbsp;
during the monologue of Emma Stone in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:39.600
where he unsuccessfully tried to&nbsp;
recreate the 2002 film's iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:43.620
make-out scene with her in front of her&nbsp;
then-boyfriend and co-star Andrew Garfield.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.720
"He's British! America's&nbsp;
suffering record unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.820
and we outsource Spider-Man to England?"

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:56.340
Despite his importance to Miles Morales' story&nbsp;
in "Into the Spider-Verse," Jake Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:00.540
Peter B. Parker doesn't appear in "Across the&nbsp;
Spider-Verse" until towards the end, when he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:05.820
revealed to be another member of Miguel O'Hara's&nbsp;
Spider-Society. With him, Parker has taken along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:10.320
his infant daughter, Mayday Parker, who has&nbsp;
adopted her father's ability to climb walls.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:13.860
Mayday Parker is a fairly important&nbsp;
character in Marvel Comics history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:19.020
first appearing in a "What If...?" comic&nbsp;
from 1997 where Mary Jane Watson never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:23.400
loses her baby with Peter Parker. Mayday&nbsp;
Parker is born and becomes Spider-Girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.900
though in that comic Mayday's powers don't&nbsp;
develop until she's a teenager. She later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:32.220
made her official Marvel Comics debut after&nbsp;
positive fan response to the "What If...?" comic.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:36.540
Though the infant Mayday gets most of the&nbsp;
attention in "Across the Spider-Verse,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.840
other variants of Mayday also appear&nbsp;
throughout the Spider-Society sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.740
albeit ones that come from a different&nbsp;
universe than Peter B. Parker's.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:48.600
After a cameo appearance in the post-credits scene&nbsp;
of "Into the Spider-Verse," the Spider-Man from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:53.820
the 1967 ABC animated series makes a return&nbsp;
appearance in "Across the Spider-Verse."

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:59.160
This classic version of Peter Parker pops up to&nbsp;
attempt to stop Miles' escape from Nueva York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:01.560
Unfortunately, this version of Spider-Man doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.980
pose much of a threat and is almost&nbsp;
immediately dispatched by Miles.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:10.080
This Peter Parker isn't even the only Spider-Man&nbsp;
who debuted on TV to make an appearance in "Across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:14.400
the Spider-Verse." The Japanese Spider-Man&nbsp;
from the 1978 Japanese live-action series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:19.860
also shows up as part of the Spider-Society,&nbsp;
accompanied by Josh Keaton's version of Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:23.580
Parker from "The Spectacular Spider-Man."&nbsp;
And in a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:28.200
the Spider-Man from "Spider-Man: Unlimited"&nbsp;
can be seen chasing Miles, web cape and all.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:32.280
If you've been watching your Spider-Man movies,&nbsp;
then you probably noticed some familiar moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:35.760
from your favorite web-slinger. Or&nbsp;
web-slingers, as the case may be.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:40.440
During one sequence where Miles, Gwen,&nbsp;
and Pavitr attempt to stop The Spot from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:45.480
wreaking havoc in Mumbattan, Pavitr rescues a&nbsp;
bus full of citizens, including his girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:49.620
from falling off a bridge. You might notice&nbsp;
that the scene closely resembles the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:55.200
in 2002's "Spider-Man," where Spider-Man rescues&nbsp;
a group of citizens, including Mary Jane Watson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:00.000
from the Roosevelt Island Tramway. It pops up&nbsp;
again in "Spider-Man: Homecoming," when Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.380
saves his classmates, including his romantic&nbsp;
interest Liz, from the Washington Monument.

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:07.740
Another iconic Spider-Man move&nbsp;
that's referenced in "Across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:11.040
Spider-Verse" is Tom Holland's trying to&nbsp;
hold a split ferry together in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:15.060
Homecoming," which is referenced by a&nbsp;
lone Spider-Man in Nueva York making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.420
himself into a bridge that a crowd&nbsp;
of Spider-People try to run across.

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:23.340
The ending of "Across the Spider-Verse" features&nbsp;
some familiar faces for audience members who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:28.080
loved "Into the Spider-Verse." Abandoned by&nbsp;
the Spider-Society, Gwen Stacy realizes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:32.280
Miles has ended up in the wrong universe, and&nbsp;
recruits a team of Spider-People to help rescue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:36.420
him from the clutches of his alternate self. That&nbsp;
group includes "Into the Spider-Verse" characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:42.180
like Peni Parker, Peter Porker, and Spider-Man&nbsp;
Noir, as well as Peter B. Parker and Mayday.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:45.360
However, Peni Parker's mech has&nbsp;
received some upgrades since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:47.700
last time audiences saw her&nbsp;
in "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:52.080
"I'm from New York in the year 3145. I&nbsp;
have a psychic link with a spider who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:55.920
lives inside my father's robot.&nbsp;
And we're best friends forever."

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:00.780
In that film, her mech resembled an actual&nbsp;
spider, but this time around, it bears a closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:05.160
resemblance to Peni Parker's original mech design&nbsp;
from her "Edge of Spider-Verse" comic debut.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:10.200
In turn, the comic book version of the mech is&nbsp;
its own Easter egg. The storyline of Peni Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:15.300
drew lots of inspiration from "Neon Genesis&nbsp;
Evangelion", a Japanese anime centered on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:20.040
group of teenagers who pilot a weaponized&nbsp;
mech, just like Peni. According to artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:24.720
Jake Wyatt, he included several characters&nbsp;
from "Neon Genesis Evangelion" as Peni's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:28.260
classmates in the "Edge of Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
comic as a tribute to their inspiration.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:32.820
The twist ending of "Across the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
finds Miles mistakenly ending up in the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:36.720
universe after using Miguel O'Hara's&nbsp;
technology to send himself home. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:40.440
the spider that bit him was from&nbsp;
Earth-42 rather than Earth-1610,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:44.520
Miles ends up in a version of his world&nbsp;
where there is no Spider-Man. Even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:48.000
the Miles of this world has been raised&nbsp;
by his uncle Aaron to become the Prowler.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.960
What truly changes things for Miles is when&nbsp;
Aaron takes him to the roof of their building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:55.500
and Miles sees a graffiti tribute&nbsp;
to his father, Jefferson Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.500 --> 00:21:00.600
instead of the tribute to Aaron in his universe.&nbsp;
If you were shocked by this twist, you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:04.620
been too distracted to notice a subtle nod to&nbsp;
the creators of Miles Morales in this tribute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:09.960
Spray-painted in faint green ink near Jefferson&nbsp;
Davis' graffiti memorial are the words "Bendis"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:15.060
and "Pichelli," which refer to writer Brian&nbsp;
Michael Bendis and illustrator Sara Pichelli.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:18.900
The two creators of Miles Morales are&nbsp;
probably used to being referenced in this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:22.380
since they also appeared in Miles' phone&nbsp;
contacts in "Into the Spider-Verse."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:26.640
Bendis and Pichelli are also referenced&nbsp;
in "Spider-Man: Homecoming" in the files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:31.560
for Donald Glover's Aaron Davis, with one of&nbsp;
his criminal aliases being "Brian Pichelli."

